  "step_2": "The individual reports comparing probabilities and losses. They note that in Lottery B, a loss is very likely, and they considered whether it was worth risking the higher loss. They explicitly state that they chose Lottery A because the probability of no loss (0 Euros) is over 50% in A — however, this is incorrect: in Lottery A, the chance of 0 Euros is only 10%, not over 50%. They seem to have confused the probabilities. They contrast this with B having only 10% chance of no loss — which is also incorrect, as B has 55% chance of no loss. Despite these errors, the gist of their reasoning is focusing on the probability of avoiding a loss (i.e., getting 0 Euros) and preferring the option with higher chance of no loss. They intended to choose based on higher probability of the best outcome (0 Euros), even though they misremembered the probabilities. Their justification centers on the likelihood of avoiding any loss, not on skewness per se, but on a specific favorable outcome (0 Euros).",
  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on assessing the overall skewness of the distribution — i.e., where most of the probability mass lies relative to favorable outcomes. In this case, Lottery B is more left-skewed because it assigns higher probability (55%) to the best outcome (0) than Lottery A (10%). The reason would prefer B. The individual, however, believes that A has a higher chance of no loss (>50%) and B only 10%, which is the opposite of the truth. They justify choosing A based on this (incorrect) belief about the probability of zero loss. While their intuitive focus on the probability of the best outcome aligns somewhat with the concept of skewness, they misperceive the actual probabilities. More importantly, they do not discuss the distribution shape, trade-offs, or skewness — only the (misstated) likelihood of avoiding loss. There is no evidence they computed or considered skewness as a structural property. Their reasoning is outcome-focused (0 vs. loss), not distribution-shaped-based. Therefore, although there is a superficial similarity in focusing on favorable outcomes, the correct information was not used, and the rule of preferring the more left-skewed lottery was not applied. The individual likely relied on a heuristic about avoiding losses with higher probability, but due to factual errors and absence of skewness-based reasoning, it cannot be concluded they used this specific reason.",
